<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Ottoman Centuries</h1><p class="path-tagline">The Ottoman state from a frontier beylik in Anatolia to the Republic of Turkey.</p><p class="path-intro">The Ottoman Empire lasted over six centuries, from about 1299 to 1922, and at its height ruled across three continents. These stops follow it from Osman I's beylik on the Byzantine frontier through the conquest of Constantinople, the gains of Selim I and the reign of Suleiman the Magnificent. They then turn to the reforms of the nineteenth century, the Tanzimat and the Young Turks, and to the defeat in the First World War that ended the sultanate in 1922. The last stops show the Republic proclaimed in Ankara in 1923 under Mustafa Kemal.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Strategist</a> · <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a> · <a href="/start">the Builder</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/ottoman-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suleymaniye-mosque.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 1299 CE – 1922 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Ottoman Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">A frontier beylik in Anatolia grew, with Janissary infantry, gunpowder artillery and a sophisticated bureaucracy, into a power spanning three continents for six centuries.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Ottoman Empire was a Turkish Muslim state founded by Osman I around 1300 in north-western Anatolia, which grew to rule south-eastern Europe, the Middle East and North Africa.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/osman-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/osman-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Person · d. c. 1324</span><span class="path-step-name">Osman I</span><span class="path-step-why">Osman I, a Turkoman chieftain of the Kayı clan, held a beylik in Bithynia on the Byzantine frontier, and his victory at Bapheus in 1301 gave the dynasty its military credibility.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Osman I was the founder of the Ottoman dynasty, a Turkoman chieftain of the Kayı clan of the Oghuz Turks, active around 1300.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1453</span><span class="path-step-name">Fall of Constantinople</span><span class="path-step-why">In 1453 Mehmed II took the Byzantine capital after a siege in which heavy artillery played a major role, ending the Byzantine Empire and making the city the Ottoman capital.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The fall of Constantinople was the capture of the Byzantine capital by the Ottoman sultan Mehmed II in 1453, after a siege in which heavy artillery played a major role.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/selim-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1470–1520</span><span class="path-step-name">Selim I</span><span class="path-step-why">Selim I defeated the Safavids at Chaldiran in 1514 and conquered the Mamluk Sultanate in 1516–1517, bringing Syria, Egypt and the holy cities of Arabia under Ottoman rule.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Selim I was Ottoman sultan from 1512 to 1520.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1494–1566</span><span class="path-step-name">Suleiman the Magnificent</span><span class="path-step-why">Suleiman, known in Turkish as Kanuni, the Lawgiver, conquered Belgrade, won at Mohács and besieged Vienna, in a reign treated as the height of Ottoman power and legal codification.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Suleiman the Magnificent, known in Turkish as Kanuni, the Lawgiver, was Ottoman sultan from 1520 to 1566.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/janissaries.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Institution</span><span class="path-step-name">Janissaries</span><span class="path-step-why">First recruited from Christian boys through the devshirme, the elite infantry became one of Europe's first standing armies to use firearms on a large scale, until their abolition in 1826.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Janissaries were the elite infantry corps of the Ottoman Empire, originally recruited from Christian boys through the devshirme system.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/movement/tanzimat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tanzimat.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 1839 CE – 1876 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Tanzimat</span><span class="path-step-why">From the Edict of Gülhane in 1839 to the constitution of 1876, Ottoman statesmen rebuilt the ministries, courts, schools, law codes and army and promised equality to all subjects.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Tanzimat was a period of reform in the Ottoman Empire from 1839 to 1876.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/movement/young-turks"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enver-pasha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · 1889 CE – 1918 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Young Turks</span><span class="path-step-why">Their Committee of Union and Progress forced the sultan to restore the constitution in 1908, later ruled as a dictatorship and masterminded the genocides of Ottoman Christians.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1881–1938</span><span class="path-step-name">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</span><span class="path-step-why">After the Ottoman defeat and the Allied occupation of Istanbul, Mustafa Kemal, an Ottoman army officer, led the Turkish national movement against the post-war partition of Anatolia.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk was an Ottoman army officer who led the Turkish national movement after the First World War and became the founding president of the Republic of Turkey.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/republic-of-turkey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · 1923 CE – 2025 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Republic of Turkey</span><span class="path-step-why">Proclaimed in Ankara on 29 October 1923 after the Treaty of Lausanne, the Republic was turned by Mustafa Kemal's reforms into a secular nation state with a new civil code and alphabet.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Republic of Turkey is the modern state founded in Anatolia after the dissolution of the Ottoman Empire following the First World War.</span></span></a></li></ol>
